Here is a simple implementation of Huffman coding in C++. This program will take a string as input and generate a Huffman tree, then encode the string using the Huffman codes.

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

struct Node {
    char ch;
    int freq;
    Node* left;
    Node* right;

    Node(char ch, int freq) : ch(ch), freq(freq), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
};

struct CompareFreq {
    bool operator()(Node* l, Node* r) {
        return l->freq > r->freq;
    }
};

void printCodes(Node* root, std::string str) {
    if (!root)
        return;

    if (root->ch != '$')
        std::cout << root->ch << ": " << str << "\n";

    printCodes(root->left, str + "0");
    printCodes(root->right, str + "1");
}

void HuffmanCodes(char data[], int freq[], int size) {
    Node *left;
    Node *right;
    Node *top;

    std::priority_queue<Node*, std::vector<Node*>, CompareFreq> minHeap;

    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i)
        minHeap.push(new Node(data[i], freq[i]));

    while (minHeap.size() != 1) {
        left = minHeap.top();
        minHeap.pop();

        right = minHeap.top();
        minHeap.pop();

        top = new Node('$', left->freq + right->freq);
        top->left = left;
        top->right = right;

        minHeap.push(top);
    }

    printCodes(minHeap.top(), "");
}

int main() {
    char arr[] = {'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f'};
    int freq[] = {5, 9, 12, 13, 16, 45};

    int size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    HuffmanCodes(arr, freq, size);

    return 0;
}
```

This program first creates a priority queue of Node pointers, where the Node struct contains a character, its frequency, and pointers to its left and right children. The CompareFreq struct is used to compare the frequencies of two nodes.

The HuffmanCodes function builds the Huffman tree by repeatedly removing the two nodes of highest priority (lowest frequency) from the queue, creating a new node with these two nodes as children and a frequency equal to the sum of the children's frequencies, and inserting the new node back into the queue. This process continues until there is only one node left in the queue, which is the root of the Huffman tree.

The printCodes function is a helper function that performs an inorder traversal of the Huffman tree and prints the Huffman code for each character. The Huffman code for a character is the concatenation of the bits on the path from the root to the character in the tree, where '0' represents a left child and '1' represents a right child.
